  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nTonight-Monday...A dry air mass remains in place across the \nFlorida peninsula tonight through Monday. PWATs generally range \nbetween 0.8- 1.2\", with some pockets of moisture building slightly\non Monday up to 1.6\". Overall, this drier air will continue to \nlimit rain and storm chances across east central Florida, with no \nmentionable chances through Monday. Light and variable winds \npersist through tonight and early Monday, becoming slightly more \nonshore during the afternoon hours as a weak, diffuse sea breeze \ndevelops and moves inland. Mostly clear skies continue through \ntonight and into Monday, with afternoon temperatures staying \nseasonable in the mid 80s. Overnight, temperatures fall into the \n60s.\n\nLong period swells will support a continued high risk of rip \ncurrents at all east central Florida beaches tonight and into \nMonday. Entering the ocean is not advised!\n\nTuesday-Saturday...A mid-level trough drifts southward towards \nthe Florida peninsula through the middle of the week, eventually \nbecoming a broad closed low across the eastern Gulf. Mid-level \nhigh pressure across the Atlantic drifts westward towards the \npeninsula by Thursday, pushing the low farther into the Gulf \nthrough late week. At the surface, the broad high pressure \nsituated across the peninsula lifts northward as an area of low \npressure out across the Atlantic far east of the Carolinas lifts \nnortheastward, slowly dragging a stalled frontal boundary \nnorthward across Florida. By mid to late week, an additional broad\ndisturbance develops near Cuba, drifting northwestward into the \nGulf. Overall, this set-up will result in gradually increasing \nmoisture across east central Florida, further aided by persistent \nonshore flow advecting moist air from the Atlantic. Isolated to \nscattered rain and storm chances return to the Treasure Coast and \nareas around Lake Okeechobee on Tuesday, spreading northward and \nencompassing the entire forecast area from Wednesday onward. A \nmajority of the activity is anticipated to follow a diurnal \npattern, with daytime heating supporting the development of a \ndiffuse sea breeze each afternoon. Intrusion inland and additional\nlocal boundary interactions will further support development, \nwith showers and storms diminishing into the overnight period. \nWhile it remains too early to pinpoint exact storm threats, \nlightning, gusty winds, and heavy downpours all look to be \npossible at this time. It is important to note that while activity\nis anticipated to diminish during the overnight hours, the \npersistent onshore flow will support low chances (20-30%) of \nisolated onshore- moving showers through the overnight and early \nmorning hours along the coast. Scattered shower and storm \ndevelopment will remain possible across the local Atlantic waters \nthrough the overnight hours.\n\nThe taste of fall across east central Florida this weekend will \nunfortunately be brief, with temperatures forecast to trend a bit \nwarmer. The warmest temperatures are anticipated across the \ninterior (upper 80s to low 90s), with the onshore flow keeping \nplaces along the coast a bit closer to normal (mid to upper 80s). \nThe increasing moisture also will push dewpoints higher, resulting\nin a return of peak heat indices into the upper 90s and even near\n100 by late week. Overnight temperatures will also trend near to \nslightly above normal through the extended.",
